Docket 1.3.112

**Docket is free to try: run a full audit of any site without a key and see your score, how many problems each area has, and the most important problem in full with its fix. [Buy it once](https://kerr-affiliate-hub.kerrco.workers.dev/buy/docket?src=release-notes), $349, or 4 payments of $87.25, to see all the others, the ranked fix plan and the markup to paste. 30-day refund, no conditions.**

- *A Cloudflare site's bot check is no longer called a refused AI crawler.* Docket asks your server for the
  homepage as each AI crawler. Cloudflare checks real crawlers by the network address they come from, so to
  Cloudflare a request from your own computer that names GPTBot or Googlebot is a crawler pretending, and it may
  challenge or turn it away while serving the real crawler normally. Docket used to report that as AI crawlers
  refused by your server, sometimes as a critical problem that was costing you search too. Now, when Cloudflare
  challenges the request, or refuses Docket's Googlebot request as well, Docket says it could not test those
  crawlers from here and tells you where the real answer is: Cloudflare's security events, and Search Console's
  URL Inspection for Google. A Cloudflare rule that turns away the AI crawlers by name while Googlebot is served is
  still reported as a block.
- *A crawler asked to pay is no longer counted as served.* A server using Cloudflare's pay per crawl answers an AI
  crawler that has not paid with HTTP 402. Docket used to count that as the page being served. It now says which
  crawlers were asked to pay, and that whether the real ones pay is something only your CDN's records show.

- *Twelve wrong findings, found by auditing two real sites,* a physiotherapy clinic and an animal-shelter
  charity, and reading every finding against the page:
  - a 1x1 tracking pixel is no longer counted as an image with no alt text;
  - the web agency that built a site is no longer read as a second spelling of the business's name;
  - a site that shows its reviews through a reviews widget is no longer told it has none;
  - a retired AI crawler name is no longer called an open door when the current name is blocked by name;
  - images an image service delivers as AVIF are no longer counted as old formats because of their file extension;
  - scripts that cannot hold up the first paint (nomodule scripts, and scripts after the last of the content) are no
    longer counted as render-blocking;
  - a title that names the site's own town, or a branch's own area, now counts as naming a place;
  - a logo link named by its title attribute is no longer reported as having no name;
  - a headline that restates its page title in other words is no longer called a different promise;
  - another company's sitemap entries are no longer counted as this site's sitemap;
  - two pages are no longer named rivals just because a third page resembles both.

- *Nine more, from a dental practice and a restaurant:*
  - a pixel set up by its own WordPress plugin is no longer called unable to fire;
  - a hidden spam-trap field is no longer counted as a field the visitor fills in;
  - social profiles declared in structured data that a script adds are no longer called undeclared;
  - a WordPress theme's built-in gradient colours are no longer counted as the site's own colours;
  - a newsletter sign-up in a pop-up on every page now counts as email capture;
  - a gift card order form is no longer called a long enquiry form;
  - a menu is no longer measured as a page of very long sentences;
  - "we would love to hear from you" under a Contact title now reads as the same promise;
  - a map built by script on the page now counts as a map.
  - a link written with one slash after http: is followed the way a browser follows it, instead of being called dead.
  - a font declared once per weight is counted as one typeface, and a CSS keyword is no longer counted as a font;
  - images on a Shopify store's own image service are no longer called old formats: it sends browsers WebP.

99 checks across 13 areas.
